        <div id="runtime-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Runtime vs Speed: The Math Every Battery Fan Hides
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every portable battery fan is sold on its best-case runtime, and almost every best-case runtime is measured at the lowest speed with oscillation off. That is not a lie, but it is the least useful number on the box. The real question is how long the fan runs at the speed you will actually use on a 90-degree night, and you can work that out yourself with one piece of information: how many watt-hours the battery holds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watt-Hours: Volts Times Amp-Hours</h3>

          <p>
            Watt-hours are volts times amp-hours. A tool battery labeled 18V and 5.0Ah holds about 90Wh. A 20V MAX 5.0Ah pack is the same thing under a different label, because 20V is the maximum voltage and 18V is the nominal one. For built-in batteries rated in milliamp-hours, multiply by the cell voltage, usually 3.6 to 3.7V, and divide by 1,000. A 7,500mAh fan battery is about 28Wh; a 4,000mAh neck fan is about 15Wh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Divide by the Fan's Draw</h3>

          <p>
            Then divide by the fan's draw. Small battery fans run from about 2 watts on low to 10 to 25 watts on high, and the relationship is not linear: fan power rises roughly with the cube of blade speed, so doubling the speed can take several times the energy. That is why Makita's own figures for the DCF203Z go from 15.5 hours on low to 6 hours on high on the same 5Ah pack, and why the Shark FlexBreeze is rated for about 24 hours on speed 1 but about 2 hours on max.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Runtime Math at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">90Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">18V 5.0Ah tool battery</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~6W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Makita DCF203Z avg on low (90Wh / 15.5h)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~20W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DeWalt DCE512B avg on max (90Wh / ~4.5h)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">28Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">7,500mAh built-in fan battery</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan Around the Medium Setting</h3>

          <p>
            The practical rule: plan around the medium setting, not the box. If you need eight hours of sleep and your fan draws 8 watts on medium, you need at least 64Wh of usable battery, which is a 4Ah tool pack or a mid-size built-in battery. Oscillation adds a small motor load, and cold or old batteries deliver less than their label, so leave a margin of 20 to 30 percent rather than planning to run the pack flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pairing a Fan With a Larger Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Once you know the draw, pairing a fan with a larger battery gets easy. A small fan averaging 8 watts runs about 100 hours from a 1,024Wh power station at roughly 85 percent usable energy, which is why a fan is one of the best things to put on a station during a summer outage. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> covers the USB batteries that can top up the smaller fans on this list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Types of Battery Fans, and What Each Is Good For
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tool-battery fans</h3>

          <p>
            Fans from Ryobi, DeWalt, Makita, Milwaukee and other platforms run on the same packs as your drill. They are usually the best value if you already own the batteries, because you are only paying for a motor and a housing. The packs are swappable, so runtime is limited only by how many you own, and when a pack wears out you replace it without throwing the fan away. Most have hanging hooks and tough housings because they are built for jobsites.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rechargeable fans with built-in batteries</h3>

          <p>
            These range from clip-on desk fans to full pedestal fans like the Shark FlexBreeze. You get one charger, one product and no platform to buy into. The downside is the sealed battery: lithium-ion cells lose capacity over a few hundred cycles and a few years, and when they go, most of these fans go with them. Buy from a brand with a real warranty, because the bargain listings are where dead-on-arrival packs and wildly inflated capacity claims live.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Disposable-battery fans</h3>

          <p>
            Fans that take D or AA cells are the least efficient to run but the best at sitting in a closet for two years and still working. They make sense in emergency kits, for elderly relatives who will not keep a battery charged, and as the fan you hand to a neighbor during a long outage. For nightly use, rechargeable cells or a rechargeable fan are far cheaper.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12-volt DC fans</h3>

          <p>
            A 12 volt DC fan plugs into a car accessory socket or wires into an RV, boat or solar battery. These are the right tool for a portable fan for the car, a camper van or an off-grid shed. A typical small 12V clip fan draws one to two amps, or roughly 12 to 24 watts. Running one from a car battery with the engine off is fine for an hour; running it all night can leave you with a battery that will not start the engine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Neck and handheld fans</h3>

          <p>
            Rechargeable personal fans, including neck fans and handheld fans, are for moving around in heat. They are light, cheap and charge from USB, but they move a small fraction of the air of even a 7-inch desk fan. They help on a walk or at an outdoor event; they will not help you sleep through a heat wave.
          </p>
        </div>

