          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Patterns That Stand Out</h3>

          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. First, the tool-battery fans are the only ones with no hard runtime ceiling: own three packs and you have three times the runtime, with no new product to buy. Second, the fans with the most generous headline numbers all quote them at the lowest setting, so compare medium-speed or high-speed figures where a maker publishes them, and use the watt-hour math above where it does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Favor Fans With Multi-Speed Runtimes</h3>

          <p>
            If you are choosing between two fans that look similar, favor the one whose maker publishes runtimes at more than one speed. Makita and Shark both do, and it is a reliable sign that the numbers were measured rather than calculated at an unrealistic setting. Listings that give a single "up to" figure and no battery capacity are the ones that most often disappoint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Airflow vs Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, remember that airflow and runtime pull in opposite directions. The DeWalt moves the most air and has the shortest rated runtime on high; the Makita moves less and runs far longer. Neither is better in the abstract. A garage at 100 degrees needs the airflow; a bedroom at 82 degrees needs the hours.
          </p>
